// The figure that made parameters necessary.
//
// Prusinkiewicz and Lindenmayer open their section on parametric L-systems by pointing at what a
// plain one cannot do.  Every line an ordinary L-system draws is one step long, or two, or three:
// the turtle has a single `length` and the alphabet has no way to say *this* segment is shorter.
// So even an isosceles right triangle cannot be traced -- the hypotenuse is the square root of two
// times the side, and no whole number of steps ever lands on it.  You can approximate by making the
// step tiny, but then that unit has to divide every length in the model, and a single internode
// takes hundreds of symbols to draw.
//
// **A module may now carry numbers**, and a production may do arithmetic on them:
//
//     'A(s)' -> '!F(s)[+A(s / R)][/(90)-A(s / R)]'
//
// That is the book's equation (1.9).  An apex `A` carrying a size draws a segment of exactly that
// length, then puts out two smaller apices, each a factor `R` down from it.  The structure is a
// recursive subdivision of a rectangle into two similar rectangles, which needs its edges in the
// proportion of one to the square root of two -- so `R` is a shade over 1.414.  No whole number of
// steps lands on that, and there is no way to write this without parameters.
//
// **The arithmetic is ordinary arithmetic.**  `R` below is a scene value, in scope inside the
// production like any other name, which is why this language needs no equivalent of the book's
// `#define`.  A module's argument may call `sqrt`, may hold a nested call, may say anything an
// expression can say.
//
// **Where a letter ends and arithmetic begins is decided by the parentheses.**  Read `[+A(s / R)]`
// and notice that the `+` is a turn while the `/` inside is a division -- then read
// `[/(90)-A(s / R)]`, where the leading `/` is a *roll*, taking 90 as its own parameter.  The book
// warns about this directly: `+`, `&`, `^` and `/` are letters of the alphabet *and* operators, and
// which is meant depends on where it sits.  A word is read module by module -- a letter, then a
// balanced parenthesis if one follows it immediately -- so the two can never be confused.
//
// **A number may drive any of the turtle's commands, not just `F`.**  `/(90)` rolls ninety degrees
// where the `angle` in the controls would otherwise have decided, which is what gives this
// structure its depth rather than leaving it flat.  A command written bare goes on using the
// controls, so nothing written before parameters existed has changed.
//
// The book sets `R` slightly above the square root of two on purpose: at the exact value the
// structure touches itself, and a little over keeps it clear.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    no gamma
    info {
        title 'Lengths That Are Not Whole'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A branching structure whose every segment is a different, irrational length.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [2.6, 2.4, -5.2]
    look at [-0.1, 1.5, 0]
    field of view 44
}

point light { location [8, 12, -10] }

// A weaker lamp opposite, so the far side of the structure is not simply black.
point light {
    location [-9, 5, -6]
    color [0.30, 0.32, 0.38]
}

background [0.10, 0.11, 0.16]

plane {
    material {
        pigment [0.15, 0.16, 0.19]
        ambient 0.10
        specular 0.04
    }
}

// A shade over the square root of two, which is the proportion the subdivision needs.
R = 1.456

tree = lsystem {
    axiom '$A(1)'
    productions {
        'A(s)' -> '!F(s)[+A(s / R)][/(90)-A(s / R)]'
    }
    controls {
        tubes
        angle 85
        diameter 0.09
        factor 0.78
    }
}

lsystem tree {
    generations 9
    material {
        pigment [0.78, 0.66, 0.44]
        specular 0.25
        shininess 50
        ambient 0.10
    }
    scale 1.5
}
